/E (Readers often have an easier time with long vowels than short vowels. Certain letters are easily confused by young readers \(e.g., b, d\), as are certain phonemes. Noting these errors and providing continual practice and scaffolding until the reader has internalized them is important for the development of later, more complex skills. )
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [9]
